# Which environment variables get written into ~/.hermes/.env when the container boots.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL. Eight scripts read ~/.hermes/.env as a FILE, not as environment
# variables - otto_server.py:13 opens it directly and dies with FileNotFoundError if it is absent,
# which is exactly how otto-server sat in FATAL on the first working build. But a .env must never
# be baked into an image layer, so it is correctly excluded by .dockerignore. On Fly the same
# values arrive as `fly secrets`, which are environment variables. The entrypoint bridges the two.
#
# A DECLARED LIST, not a dump of the environment. Writing every variable would put PATH, HOME and
# supervisor's own internals into a file that scripts parse as credentials. The list is taken from
# the laptop's real ~/.hermes/.env on 2026-08-18.
#
# A key that is not set in the environment is simply skipped. That is deliberate: a container with
# no Telegram token should start and say so, which is what cockpit does, rather than refuse to boot.
AGENT_BROWSER_EXECUTABLE_PATH
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
BROWSERBASE_ADVANCED_STEALTH
BROWSERBASE_PROXIES
BROWSER_INACTIVITY_TIMEOUT
BROWSER_SESSION_TIMEOUT
DEEPSEEK_API_KEY
EXA_API_KEY
GEMINI_API_KEY
IMAGE_TOOLS_DEBUG
MINIMAX_API_KEY
MOA_TOOLS_DEBUG
OPENAI_API_KEY
OPENAI_BASE_URL
RSI_SIGNING_KEY
STANDARDCOMPUTE_API_KEY
STANDARD_COMPUTE_API_KEY
TELEGRAM_ALLOWED_USERS
TELEGRAM_ALLOWED_USER_IDS
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN
TELEGRAM_CRON_IN_MAIN_DM
TELEGRAM_HOME_CHANNEL
TELEGRAM_HOME_CHANNEL_THREAD_ID
TELEGRAM_WEBHOOK_SECRET
TERMINAL_LIFETIME_SECONDS
TERMINAL_MODAL_IMAGE
TERMINAL_TIMEOUT
VISION_TOOLS_DEBUG
WEB_TOOLS_DEBUG
